      {"type":"mc","q":"Which principle about converse must guide a correct analysis of “A condition becomes a valid biconditional only after both directions are proved”?","choices":["The converse of p -> q is q -> p and is not guaranteed true","The converse is not p -> not q","Every theorem has a true converse","Converse and contrapositive are equivalent"],"answer":"The converse of p -> q is q -> p and is not guaranteed true","explain":"The challenge “A condition becomes a valid biconditional only after both directions are proved” requires the converse relationship. The converse of p -> q is q -> p and is not guaranteed true"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which principle about inverse must guide a correct analysis of “Use a rectangle that is not a square as a counterexample to both converse-related statements”?","choices":["The inverse of p -> q is not p -> not q and has the same truth value as the converse","The inverse is q -> p","The inverse is equivalent to the original","Negating one part is sufficient"],"answer":"The inverse of p -> q is not p -> not q and has the same truth value as the converse","explain":"The challenge “Use a rectangle that is not a square as a counterexample to both converse-related statements” requires the inverse relationship. The inverse of p -> q is not p -> not q and has the same truth value as the converse"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which principle about contrapositive must guide a correct analysis of “Prove a geometric theorem indirectly by establishing its contrapositive”?","choices":["The contrapositive not q -> not p is logically equivalent to the original conditional","The contrapositive reverses without negating","It is equivalent only when examples agree","It must be false if the converse is false"],"answer":"The contrapositive not q -> not p is logically equivalent to the original conditional","explain":"The challenge “Prove a geometric theorem indirectly by establishing its contrapositive” requires the contrapositive relationship. The contrapositive not q -> not p is logically equivalent to the original conditional"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which principle about counterexample must guide a correct analysis of “Find coordinates satisfying the hypothesis while violating a claimed conclusion despite a misleading sketch”?","choices":["One valid counterexample disproves a universal conjecture but does not prove a different universal claim","Many confirming examples prove a theorem","A counterexample must be typical","A diagram alone is formal proof"],"answer":"One valid counterexample disproves a universal conjecture but does not prove a different universal claim","explain":"The challenge “Find coordinates satisfying the hypothesis while violating a claimed conclusion despite a misleading sketch” requires the counterexample relationship. One valid counterexample disproves a universal conjecture but does not prove a different universal claim"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which governing principle about conditional statement remains valid when “If a figure is a square, then it is a rectangle” and “Translate a theorem into hypothesis and conclusion without adding assumptions from the diagram” are evaluated together?","choices":["A conditional p -> q is false only when p is true and q is false","A conditional is false whenever p is false","p and q can be reversed without change","A conditional proves its premise"],"answer":"A conditional p -> q is false only when p is true and q is false","explain":"The application and challenge both test the same conditional statement relationship. A conditional p -> q is false only when p is true and q is false"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Which governing principle about converse remains valid when “The converse of 'square implies rectangle' is tested” and “A condition becomes a valid biconditional only after both directions are proved” are evaluated together?","choices":["The converse of p -> q is q -> p and is not guaranteed true","The converse is not p -> not q","Every theorem has a true converse","Converse and contrapositive are equivalent"],"answer":"The converse of p -> q is q -> p and is not guaranteed true","explain":"The application and challenge both test the same converse relationship. The converse of p -> q is q -> p and is not guaranteed true"}
